   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "# What to do with this\n",
    "\n",
    "**If the two quality factors agree**, you have a characterised coil, and `bench_coil_results.json`\n",
    "holds everything a later notebook needs. Stage 10's atlas can be pointed at it with\n",
    "`EMULATION = False` and the constants read from the file rather than from `reso.config()`.\n",
    "\n",
    "**If they disagree**, work through them in this order, because that is the order of how often\n",
    "each is the culprit:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **The swept fit's baseline.** It is the most fragile of the three. Look at the Step 3 plot:\n",
    "   if the fitted baseline is a sizeable fraction of the peak, the width is being inflated.\n",
    "   More drive, or a shorter pickup lead, fixes it properly; the phase method is immune to it.\n",
    "2. **Something in the band.** Compare the Step 2 survey with and without the coil. A stray tone\n",
    "   inside the receiver window sits on the fit and moves it.\n",
    "3. **Compression.** Step 6 tests this directly. If the centre or the width moves with drive\n",
    "   level, everything taken at the higher level is suspect.\n",
    "4. **The phase fit's grid.** If Step 5a declined, it now says which edge of its search it ran\n",
    "   into, and the two mean opposite things: the bottom edge points at an inflated *amplitude*\n",
    "   width (a baseline under the peak), the top edge at something in the band dragging the phase.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Things deliberately not done here**, and why:\n",
    "\n",
    "| | |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| Walking into self-oscillation | Saturates the input, and a saturated input still looks vaguely like a resonance. Worth doing, but as its own experiment with the level watched continuously |\n",
    "| Varying the coupling | The capacitors are soldered on. Step 6's linearity ladder is the available substitute; the real test needs a second pair of capacitors |\n",
    "| Anything needing an ion | No trap, no ion. The dip, resistive cooling, ion temperature and the phase-sensitive technique all wait for the trap |\n",
    "| A servo during the scan | Step 7 says whether one is needed. If the drift is under about a tenth of a linewidth per scan, it is not |\n",
    "\n",
    "**Run the self-test cell whenever a result surprises you.** It is ten seconds, it needs no\n",
    "recabling, and it separates \"the coil did something interesting\" from \"the instrument moved\" —\n",
    "which is the distinction this whole project is built around."
   ]
  }
 ],
